body:
The [close-the-dialog algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#close-the-dialog) restores `previously focused element` if non-null, but does nothing with focus when it is null. If focus is inside the dialog when it closes, this leaves the focused area inside a closed dialog until the [asynchronous focus fixup rule](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#focus-fixup-rule) runs. In the meantime, keyboard events can be lost because the active element is no longer focusable.

Reproduction:

```html
<dialog><input></dialog>
<script>
  const dialog = document.querySelector('dialog');
  const input = document.querySelector('input');
  dialog.showModal();
  input.focus();
  dialog.close();
  console.log(document.activeElement === input); // true
  setTimeout(() => console.log(document.activeElement === document.body), 150); // true
</script>
```

Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit all leave the input active immediately after `close()`, then change `document.activeElement` to `body` later. A [Chromium change](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8644932) adds a synchronous regression test for this case.

Could the dialog-close steps run the [focusing steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#focusing-steps) for the document's viewport when `previously focused element` is null **and** the focused area's DOM anchor is a shadow-including inclusive descendant of the dialog? This uses the existing focus model rather than introducing a separate notion of "clearing" focus, and preserves focus when it has already moved elsewhere.
